{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>You shall not plant for yourself an Asherah</b> — any tree planted at the entrance of a house of Elohim is called Asherah. Perhaps it is so called because it serves as a signpost, indicating the way to the people, from the language of <i>my steps have held fast to Your paths</i> (תהלים י\"ז ה') [from the root אש\"ר, meaning to step or proceed]. Scripture warns that one may not plant a tree next to the altar of YHWH as an adornment, thinking that it constitutes honor and splendor for the altar of HaShem; and He forbade it because it is the custom of idolaters to plant trees at the entrances of their temples of idolatry, as it is said: <i>and you shall tear down the altar of Baal</i>... <i>and the Asherah that is upon it you shall cut down</i> (Judges 6:25). And Rashi wrote: <i>You shall not plant for yourself an Asherah</i> — to make him liable for it from the moment of its planting; even if he did not worship it, he transgresses a negative commandment on account of planting it. <i>And you shall not plant any tree next to the altar of YHWH your Elohim</i> — a warning to one who plants and builds on the Temple Mount. If so, he would say that <i>You shall not plant for yourself an Asherah</i> and <i>any tree next to the altar of YHWH</i> [constitute the prohibition], whereas [the prohibition against] building on the Temple Mount is an asmakhta (scriptural support) derived by the Sages from their own reasoning, for Scripture forbade only planting — <i>You shall not plant for yourself an Asherah</i> and <i>You shall not plant for yourself any tree next to the altar of YHWH your Elohim</i>. [That is, the second verse is read as: \"and you shall not plant any tree\" — reading תטע rather than the ketiv תציג.]"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:33.799342600Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}